Run: IVR builder (v1) — feat/ivr-builder
Finished: 2026-07-17

## What this run accomplished

All 14 steps in PLAN.md are complete. Full v1 IVR builder shipped on
`feat/ivr-builder`, TDD throughout, everything under the dry-run rails
(no live Twilio mutation, no live AI spend):

**Backend (Steps 1-9, 34 test files / 167 tests, typecheck + lint clean):**
- Migration `0005_ivr.sql` — `cc_ivr_flows` + `cc_voicemails`.
- DB accessors (`server/db/ivr-flows.ts`, `server/db/voicemails.ts`).
- Graph model + the 5 validation rules (`server/lib/ivr/graph.ts`,
  `server/lib/ivr/validate.ts`).
- Pure interpreter core (`server/lib/ivr/interpret.ts`) — walks a flow
  graph node-by-node, no D1/Twilio I/O.
- The interpreter webhook (`POST /api/voice/ivr`) and voicemail callback
  (`POST /api/voice/voicemail`), signature-validated, mounted outside
  the AuthPak gate.
- Flow CRUD API + voicemail read API (`server/routes/ivr.ts`), org-scoped,
  cross-org access returns 404 (never 403) throughout.

**Frontend (Steps 10-13, verified by `npm run build` + `npm run lint` —
no unit-test harness for the frontend, per the plan):**
- Flow list page (`src/pages/IvrFlows.tsx`), nav entry, `@xyflow/react` dep.
- Editor canvas (`src/pages/IvrEditor.tsx` + `src/components/ivr/*`) —
  custom node components per type, a palette, drag-to-connect edges with
  branch selection, a config panel bound to the selected node/edge, Save.
- Client-side validation (`src/lib/ivr/validate.ts`) mirroring the
  server's rules live, blocking Save while invalid.
- An in-browser simulator (`src/lib/ivr/simulate.ts` +
  `SimulatorPanel.tsx`) — walk the flow with no real call, press menu
  keys, submit collect digits, toggle after-hours, see the TwiML that
  would be emitted, watch the path highlight on the canvas.

**Notable infrastructure move (Step 11):** the graph model moved to
`shared/ivr/graph.ts` (this repo already had `@shared/*` wired in
tsconfig/vite config, unused until now) so the server interpreter and
the client editor share one type definition instead of a hand-kept
duplicate. Validation logic and the simulator walker stayed deliberate
mirrors (client copies), not shared modules, since they run client-side
with no server counterpart to import from and may reasonably diverge
over time (per the design spec's own "mirrored in the client" wording).

**Docs (Step 14):** `CLAUDE.md` §6/§7 and
`docs/design/foundry-clarion-design.md` §4/§9 updated to reflect the
native-interpreter decision (superseding the original "Studio flow
reference" assumption) and the new tables.

Final gate green: `npx vitest run` (167 tests), `npm run
typecheck:server`, `npm run lint`, `npm run build` — all clean.

## What's NOT done (deliberate scope, not a blocker)

- **Attaching a flow to a real inbound number** — needs `cc_numbers` +
  live Twilio. Explicitly out of scope for v1, called out repeatedly in
  the spec as Phase 5 work.
- **Voicemail transcript read endpoint** — `cc_recordings` has one
  (`GET /api/recordings/:id/transcript`); `cc_voicemails` doesn't, because
  Step 8's plan scope literally said "list + media endpoints" only. Cheap
  follow-up if wanted — mirror the recordings transcript route.
- **Visual polish of the editor canvas** — functional, validated,
  design-token styled, but the plan's own notes flagged true polish as
  best iterated interactively with Steven rather than gold-plated
  overnight. The simulator + a clean layout were the bar for this run.
- **No human has driven the editor + simulator live in a browser yet.**
  Steps 10-13 were verified by build/typecheck/lint (the plan's own
  chosen gate, since the frontend has no unit-test harness) — nobody has
  clicked through `npm run dev` and actually built/tested a flow by hand.
  This is the natural first thing to do in the next interactive session.

